  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=-tan\u2061x$ when $x=\\pi .$",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change  when $x=\\pi $ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ at $x=\\pi .$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan\u2061x)={sec}^{2}\u2061x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-tan\u2061x) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\pi ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(\\pi ) & =-{sec}^{2}\u2061\\pi  \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate of change is $-1.$",
